## 🚀 Introduction

In the mobile-first world, users expect apps to be lightning-fast and buttery-smooth. A delay of even 1 second can cost you user retention, app store ratings, and monetization opportunities.

Let's walk through the most impactful strategies to make your React Native app feel like it's running on native steroids.

---

## 🔍 Common Performance Pain Points

Before diving into solutions, let's identify the typical performance issues in React Native apps:

| Issue | Symptoms | Impact |
|-------|----------|--------|
| **Laggy Animations** | Stuttering transitions | Poor user experience |
| **Janky Scrolling** | Frame drops while scrolling | Frustration and abandonment |
| **Slow App Launch** | Long initial load time | High bounce rate |
| **Memory Leaks** | App crashes after extended use | Negative reviews |
| **Excessive Re-renders** | UI freezes during interactions | User frustration |

---

## 🧠 Understanding the React Native Architecture

React Native runs on two threads:

1. **JavaScript Thread**: Executes your React/JS code
2. **Main/UI Thread**: Handles native UI rendering

Performance issues often occur when there's too much communication between these threads or when either thread gets blocked.

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
// This component will cause JS thread to work hard
function ExpensiveComponent() {
  // ❌ Bad: Recalculating on every render
  const expensiveCalculation = () => {
    let result = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
      result += Math.random();
    }
    return result;
  };
  
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{expensiveCalculation()}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

---

## ⚡ Optimizing JavaScript Performance

### 1. Memoize Components with React.memo

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
// ✅ Good: Prevent unnecessary re-renders
const ProductCard = React.memo(({ product }) => {
  return (
    <View style={styles.card}>
      <Image source={{ uri: product.image }} style={styles.image} />
      <Text style={styles.title}>{product.name}</Text>
      <Text style={styles.price}>${product.price}</Text>
    </View>
  );
});
```

### 2. Use useMemo and useCallback Hooks

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
function ProductScreen({ category }) {
  // ✅ Good: Memoize expensive calculations
  const filteredProducts = useMemo(() => {
    return products.filter(p => p.category === category);
  }, [category]);
  
  // ✅ Good: Memoize event handlers
  const handleFavorite = useCallback((productId) => {
    dispatch(addToFavorites(productId));
  }, [dispatch]);
  
  return (
    <FlatList
      data={filteredProducts}
      renderItem={({ item }) => (
        <ProductCard 
          product={item} 
          onFavorite={() => handleFavorite(item.id)}
        />
      )}
    />
  );
}
```

### 3. Optimize List Rendering

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
// ✅ Good: Optimized FlatList configuration
<FlatList
  data={products}
  renderItem={renderProduct}
  keyExtractor={item => item.id.toString()}
  getItemLayout={(data, index) => ({
    length: ITEM_HEIGHT,
    offset: ITEM_HEIGHT * index,
    index,
  })}
  removeClippedSubviews={true}
  maxToRenderPerBatch={10}
  updateCellsBatchingPeriod={50}
  windowSize={5}
  initialNumToRender={5}
/>
```

---

## 🖼️ Optimizing UI Rendering

### 1. Use Native Driver for Animations

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
import { Animated } from 'react-native';

// ✅ Good: Animation runs on UI thread
const fadeAnim = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current;

Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
  toValue: 1,
  duration: 500,
  useNativeDriver: true, // This is key!
}).start();

return (
  <Animated.View style={{ opacity: fadeAnim }}>
    <Text>Fading In</Text>
  </Animated.View>
);
```

### 2. Optimize Image Loading

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
// ✅ Good: Progressive image loading
import FastImage from 'react-native-fast-image';

function OptimizedImage({ uri, style }) {
  return (
    <FastImage
      style={style}
      source={{
        uri: uri,
        priority: FastImage.priority.normal,
      }}
      resizeMode={FastImage.resizeMode.cover}
    />
  );
}
```

### 3. Use PureComponent or shouldComponentUpdate

```jsx
# jsx
# copy
# edit
// ✅ Good: Only update when props change
class ExpensiveComponent extends React.PureComponent {
  render() {
    return (
      <View>
        <Text>{this.props.value}</Text>
      </View>
    );
  }
}

// Alternative with function components
function ExpensiveComponent({ value }) {
  console.log('Rendering ExpensiveComponent');
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>{value}</Text>
    </View>
  );
}

export default React.memo(ExpensiveComponent);
```

